  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\"><em>The Point of Philosophy</em>, published by Peter Lang Verlag in 2016 as part of the established Philosophie et Politique series, has received one documented citation as of the available record. The work explores the intersection of geometry and epistemology, positioning itself within philosophical inquiry into foundational concepts. Library holdings indicate modest institutional uptake, with the book present in at least four library systems and catalogued under philosophy history and social sciences.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Authored by Ludo Abicht and Hendrik Opdebeeck, <em>The Point of Philosophy</em> engages with the concept of \"point\" as both a geometric and philosophical category. The work's conceptual signature\u2014anchored in geometry, philosophy, and epistemology\u2014suggests an interdisciplinary treatment of how foundational mathematical concepts relate to broader questions of knowledge and meaning. This positioning reflects the intellectual scope of its home series, which has published philosophical work on democracy, ethics, ontology, and social theory over more than a decade.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Citation evidence for this volume remains limited but suggestive of early scholarly engagement. The single recorded citation, appearing in 2017 in a book chapter titled \"Integral Ecology and the Anthropocene,\" was authored by Opdebeeck himself. This citation chain indicates that at least one of the book's ideas or arguments proved sufficiently generative to warrant return and extension by its own authors in subsequent work\u2014a form of internal intellectual development common in sustained research programs.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Library infrastructure provides a secondary signal of reach. The work appears in WorldCat and holds positions in at least four institutional collections, each cataloguing it under philosophy history and philosophy's relationship to social sciences. This distribution suggests placement in research libraries and academic collections, consistent with scholarly monograph adoption patterns in humanities publishing.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Publication through Peter Lang Verlag situates the volume within established European scholarly publishing networks. The Philosophie et Politique series itself continues to issue work by philosophers engaged with normative and conceptual questions\u2014including Opdebeeck's own 2019 contribution on economy and meaningfulness\u2014suggesting that <em>The Point of Philosophy</em> occupies a stable position within a coherent intellectual community, even if broader citation accumulation remains modest at the time this record was generated.</p>",
